  "name": "Apolipoprotein B (APO-B)",
  "code": "APOB",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<table><thead><tr><th><div>Apo B Level (mg/dL)</div></th><th><div>Interpretation</div></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><div>Below Normal Range</div></td><td><div>Low Apo B levels are less common and may be associated with certain genetic disorders or conditions affecting lipid metabolism. Further evaluation is necessary to identify the underlying cause and appropriate management.</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>Normal Range</div></td><td><div>Apo B levels within the normal range are considered optimal for cardiovascular health.</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>Above Normal Range</div></td><td><div>High Apo B levels may indicate an increased risk of cardiovascular diseases, such as coronary artery disease. Elevated levels are associated with higher levels of LDL cholesterol, which is considered a risk factor for heart disease. Further evaluation and lifestyle interventions may be necessary to manage cardiovascular risk.</div></td></tr></tbody></table>",
